"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/0c8d3dd9-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Routing and the role of the seeds {#routing-and-the-role-of-the-seeds}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The metrics for routing are the **two-qubit depth** and the **two-qubit gate count** after the preset pass manager at\n"
"optimization level 3, in a given basis. The default basis in this tutorial is `{cz, rz, sx, x}`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Which part is random?* The layout is fixed, but the router is not deterministic across seeds. The Qiskit `SabreSwap` pass runs\n"
"several randomized trials (20 at optimization level 3), seeded from the `seed_transpiler` value that you pass, and keeps the trial with\n"
"the fewest SWAP gates. Therefore, a seed fixes the whole set of trials, and a different seed gives a different set. The router has\n"
"work to do on every layout, because of the `ZZ` pairs that the previous section counted. Different seeds find different SWAP gates, and the\n"
"router selects by SWAP count, not by two-qubit depth. For these reasons, the code routes every circuit with 20 seeds and **keeps the best result**: the\n"
"smallest two-qubit depth, with ties going to the smaller gate count. The gate count that this tutorial reports is the count of that same circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Fractional gates: a second gate set {#fractional-gates-a-second-gate-set}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"IBM Heron and Nighthawk devices can run `rzz(theta)` for $0 < \\theta \\le \\pi/2$ and `rx(theta)` for any $\\theta$ as native\n"
"gates. These *fractional gates* are experimental and are off by default. On a real backend, you turn them on with\n"
"`QiskitRuntimeService.backend(\"ibm_torino\", use_fractional_gates=True)`. This setting is a *backend* option, not a transpiler flag.\n"
"The returned `Target` contains `rzz` with `angle_bounds=[(0, pi/2)]`, and `rx`, and the transpiler then uses them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The code routes every circuit on the QPUs twice: onto the plain `{cz, rz, sx, x}` default basis, and\n"
"onto a fractional target. A two-qubit layer is a layer of `cz` **or** `rzz` gates, and each of these gates counts as one two-qubit gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`FakeFez` and `FakeNighthawk` in this environment do not include fractional gates, so the next cell **builds the targets manually**,\n"
"based on the assumptions that are documented in the cell. The depths that later cells report therefore show what the transpiler can do with\n"
"such a gate set. They do not show what a real backend with fractional gates delivers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Route onto the QPUs, in both gate sets {#route-onto-the-qpus-in-both-gate-"
"sets}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code routes the flow-set circuit onto Heron and onto Nighthawk"
" with the chains from the previous section. For comparison, it also routes "
"the `LieTrotter` circuit and the flow-set circuit onto a plain line.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/47dd8196-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Solid lines are the `{cz, rz, sx, x}` basis, and dashed lines are the "
"fractional gate set. The two gray lines are the plain line.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The synthesis.** With `LieTrotter`, the depth grows with every site (from 47 at $L = 4$ to 599 at $L = 50$, or 12 layers per site).\n"
"The flow-set synthesis stays between 20 and 25 over the same range. The bands reach from the best to the worst of the 20 seeds.\n"
"The gate count grows linearly for both syntheses, and is lower for the flow-set synthesis (882 compared with 1032 at $L = 50$). Therefore, the lower\n"
"depth does not cost extra gates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The QPUs.** On both QPUs, the flow-set circuit has the same low depth as on the line. The best depth is between 20 and 25 for $L$\n"
"from 4 to 50 on Heron, on Nighthawk, and on the line. The gate counts are also similar. At $L = 10$, the circuit has 164 two-qubit gates on\n"
"Heron, 162 on Nighthawk, and 164 on the line. At $L = 50$, it has 880, 838, and 882. Therefore, **any hardware that has a long\n"
"enough path can run this circuit at an almost constant depth**. The layout is a simple search on Heron, or a snake through\n"
"the rows on Nighthawk.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Fractional gates.** The fractional gate set lowers the best depth by six layers at every size. On Heron, the depth goes from 20, 20, 23, 23,\n"
"23, 25 to 14, 14, 17, 17, 17, 19. On Nighthawk, it goes from 20, 22, 22, 22, 22, 25 to 14, 16, 16, 16, 16, 19 (at $L = 4, 10, 20, 30,\n"
"40, 50$). This result is expected. The `YY` and `ZZ` flow sets consist of `rzz` rotations, which the fractional gate set runs\n"
"as one gate, while the `cz` basis needs two `cz` gates. These rotations cause the constant drop in depth, and a drop in gate count that\n"
"increases with $L$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/af0aefae-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following drawing shows one of the routed circuits, for $L = 10$ on Heron (the best of the seeds, from the previous results).\n"
"Each wire is labeled `q_i ↦ p`, which means that virtual qubit `i` of the encoded circuit starts on physical qubit `p` of the device.\n"
"For Nighthawk, `p = 10 * row + col`. The drawing shows only the qubits that the circuit acts on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"5. Connectivity of Nighthawk devices {#5-connectivity-of-nighthawk-devices}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The interleaved circuit needs only a path. Suppose the QPU offers more connectivity. The qubits of Nighthawk are on a square\n"
"lattice, and a square lattice contains a **ladder**: two parallel chains whose rungs are real couplers. A ladder is the\n"
"interaction graph of the Hubbard model in blocked order. The spin-up chain is on one rail, the spin-down chain is on the other, and the on-site term acts\n"
"across a rung.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The single-chain encoding of section 4 cannot use the ladder. It places all $2L$ modes along **one** chain. With blocked order,\n"
"the spin-up and spin-down blocks are joined end to end, and the on-site plaquettes stretch across the join. Instead, give\n"
"**each spin its own chain and its own ancilla**:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Spin-up modes $0..L-1$ are on qubits $0..L-1$, with the ancilla on qubit "
"$L$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Spin-down modes $L..2L-1$ are on qubits $L+1..2L$, with the ancilla on qubit"
" $2L+1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each block uses the same images as before: $V_j = Z_qZ_{q+1}$, $T_{j,j+1} = -X_{q+1}/2$, and $T_{j+1,j} = \\tfrac12 Z_qX_{q+1}Z_{q+2}$,\n"
"where $q$ is the qubit of mode $j$. The total is $2L + 2$ qubits, which is one more than before.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every flow set is now local on the ladder. The `X` and `ZXZ` terms act along a rail, and the interaction `ZZZZ @ (0, 1, L+1, L+2)` is a 2x2\n"
"plaquette (two neighbors on one rail and the two qubits next to them on the other rail).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the encoded space, spin-up and spin-down operators now *commute* instead of anticommuting. This difference has no effect on this\n"
"Hamiltonian, because it contains only bilinears of the same spin and products of vertex operators. An operator with hopping between spins\n"
"raises an error in the action, so it cannot silently give a wrong answer. Perform the same check against\n"
"the known `LieTrotter` circuit as for the single-ancilla interleaved ordering:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A ladder on the QPU {#a-ladder-on-the-qpu}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A chain of $L + 1$ qubits fits on Nighthawk in a straight line if it has at most 12 qubits (along the 12 rows) or at most 10\n"
"(along the 10 columns). Therefore, up to $L = 11$, the two chains are side by side on two neighboring grid lines, rung by rung, and\n"
"every coupler that the circuit needs is a real coupler. The `folded_ladder` function returns this layout. For larger $L$, it also folds the ladder,\n"
"as section 6 describes. The function also selects the orientation that needs fewer strips, and selects the horizontal orientation if both need the same number.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following drawings show both straight cases. For $L = 8$, a chain of 9 qubits fits along the 10 columns, so the ladder is\n"
"horizontal. For $L = 10$, a chain of 11 qubits does not fit along the 10 columns (it needs two strips), but it fits along\n"
"the 12 rows, so the layout uses a vertical ladder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/25d1a8f2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code compares the two orderings at the sizes that fit natively: interleaved on the snake, and blocked on the ladder, both on Nighthawk and in\n"
"both gate sets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For the blocked ladder that fits natively on the QPU ($L \\leq 10$), no gates are off a coupler.\n"
"Therefore, for these sizes, the routing step is not expected to change the circuit statistics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/b8e745c2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At the sizes that fit natively ($L \\le 10$), the blocked circuit is **about half as deep**. At $L = 10$, its depth is 10 compared with 22 in the `cz` basis, and\n"
"8 compared with 16 with fractional gates. At $L = 4$, the values are 10 compared with 20, and 8 compared with 14. The depth of the blocked circuit does not change\n"
"between $L = 4$ and $L = 10$. The blocked circuit also needs fewer gates. At $L = 10$, it needs 82 compared with 162 in the `cz` basis, and 72 compared with 116 with\n"
"fractional gates. These results are expected, because no gates are off a coupler.\n"
"Therefore, one more ancilla qubit reduces the depth by a factor of two for these sizes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At $L = 12$, this advantage ends. A chain of 13 qubits does not fit on a straight grid line, so the ladder must bend. Then six of the\n"
"98 two-qubit gates are off a coupler, and the depth of the blocked circuit increases to 25 in the `cz` basis and 23 with fractional gates. These depths are\n"
"*greater* than the interleaved depths at the same size (22 and 16). The next section examines that fold.\n"
"As before, you can also view the routed circuit of the blocked encoding for $L = 10$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/ba54a995-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"6. Scaling beyond the native dimensions of Nighthawk {#6-scaling-beyond-the-"
"native-dimensions-of-nighthawk}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For $L$ greater than 11, a chain does not fit in a straight line, so the ladder must **fold**. The `folded_ladder` function cuts each chain into segments, and\n"
"stacks the segments in strips of two adjacent grid lines. Each strip starts at the opposite end from the end of the previous strip.\n"
"Each strip boundary stretches one spin-up link and one spin-down link from distance 1 to distance 2, and this stretch is the only imperfection.\n"
"Rungs stay real couplers everywhere. A ladder cannot bend on a square lattice without that stretch, so fewer strips are\n"
"better. The strips can run along the 12 rows instead of the 10 columns, which needs fewer strips, and `folded_ladder` selects\n"
"the orientation that needs fewer strips (the orientation changes between $L=49$ and $L=50$). In the following drawings, the stretched links are red and dotted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/c35f19ce-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code shows the cost of the fold. It runs the same comparison as before, up to 50 sites, and adds blocked order on Heron.\n"
"The heavy-hex lattice has no ladder, so on Heron the router chooses the layout. This comparison is the final comparison of all four\n"
"variants, with the depth on the left and the two-qubit gate count on the right.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/9756fef7-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Between $L = 10$ and $L = 12$, the depth of the blocked circuit increases from 10 to 25 in the `cz` basis (from eight to 23 with fractional\n"
"gates), and then stays the same up to $L = 50$. **The fold increases the depth once, not once per strip.** The drawing for $L = 50$ has four\n"
"folds and eight stretched links, and the depth is the same as at $L = 12$, which has one fold. The depth stays the same because the\n"
"depth penalties of all folds occur in parallel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Result of the comparison.** Beyond the width of the QPU, blocked order loses its depth advantage. At $L = 50$, both orderings\n"
"reach 25 in the `cz` basis. The best interleaved seed reaches 22 between $L = 12$ and $L = 40$, but its median is 23 to 25.\n"
"With fractional gates, blocked order (23) is deeper than interleaved order (19). Blocked order still has a lower gate count: 462 compared with 838\n"
"in the `cz` basis (45% fewer) and 412 compared with 592 with fractional gates (30% fewer), at the cost of one more qubit. Fewer gates\n"
"can mean less accumulated error on a real device, which can be a reason to prefer blocked order on a QPU with a ladder. The\n"
"observable that you measure at the end of the circuit can also affect the choice of encoding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**On Heron, which has no ladder, blocked order is much worse.** Its best depth grows from 29 at $L = 4$ to 95 at $L = 50$\n"
"in the `cz` basis (92 with fractional gates), and the median and worst seeds at $L = 50$ give 134 and 166.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "7. Beyond one dimension {#7-beyond-one-dimension}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All of the previous sections are one-dimensional, where the hardware needs to hold only a path or a ladder. A square lattice of sites is\n"
"the case where the square connectivity of Nighthawk matters most, so this section briefly examines it. To keep the section short, it omits spin\n"
"and uses the **spinless** model of the [Simulate 2D Fermi-Hubbard dynamics with flow sets](/addons/qiskit-fermions/guides/2-d-fermi-hubbard) guide\n"
"(Gandon et al., arXiv:2512.11418). That model has hopping and interaction on every nearest-neighbor bond of an $R \\times C$ lattice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the Verstraete-Cirac encoding of the model, every site gets **its own ancilla** ($N = RC$ modes use $2N$ qubits), and the hopping splits into **four flow\n"
"sets** (east, west, north, and south). A Clifford circuit of two-qubit depth 2, which acts on all rows or all columns\n"
"at the same time, diagonalizes each flow set. As in 1D, the depth of the circuit therefore does not grow with the lattice. The guide shows each of these steps, but it\n"
"does not transpile to a given connectivity. The following drawing shows how the model fits on Nighthawk.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The stripe layout on Nighthawk.** Lattice row $r$ runs down QPU column $r$ as a stripe that alternates site, ancilla, site,\n"
"ancilla (lattice column $c$ is on QPU rows $2c$ and $2c+1$). A site and its own ancilla are neighbors, an ancilla and the *next*\n"
"site along the row are neighbors, and vertical neighbors are on adjacent QPU columns. Therefore, **all hopping terms are on couplers**, and\n"
"the Clifford circuits need no routing. The interaction between two sites of the same row is not native, because the two sites are two QPU qubits\n"
"apart, with the ancilla between them (the red arcs). These pairs are the same $\\texttt{ZZ}$-type pairs that you routed in 1D, with one `rzz` gate per\n"
"horizontal bond, so the routing cost is similar to the cost in 1D. The largest lattice that fits this layout is $10 \\times 6$\n"
"(60 sites, which use all 120 qubits).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/spinful-fermi-"
"hubbard-dynamics/extracted-outputs/b789431d-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "8. Takeaways and next steps {#8-takeaways-and-next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code summarizes the circuit cost of a single Trotter step with"
" the flow-set synthesis, for each QPU connectivity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These results lead to the following high-level takeaways:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**On Nighthawk, use blocked order with two ancillas.** While the ladder fits on the QPU, its depth is half the depth of\n"
"interleaved order (10 compared with 22 in the `cz` basis at $L = 10$). After the fold, its depth stays at 25 (23 with fractional gates).\n"
"Interleaved order has an equally constant depth in the `cz` basis and a lower depth with fractional gates (25 and 19 at $L = 50$). Therefore, at large sizes,\n"
"interleaved order has a small advantage in depth. The blocked circuit still needs far fewer two-qubit gates: 462\n"
"compared with 838 in the `cz` basis (about 45% fewer), and 412 compared with 592 with fractional gates (about 30% fewer), at the cost of\n"
"one more qubit. Fewer gates can mean less accumulated error on a real device, which can be a reason to prefer blocked order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Without square connectivity, use interleaved order.** One ancilla and any path through the device give a constant depth on Heron\n"
"too (25 in the `cz` basis and 19 with fractional gates at $L = 50$). On Heron, the blocked variant has no ladder to use. Its best\n"
"depth grows with $L$ (95 and 92 at $L = 50$, and 166 and 161 for the worst routing seed), and fractional gates barely reduce it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Fractional gates lower the constant depth.** Where the depth is constant, it drops from 25 to 19 for both interleaved variants and\n"
"from 25 to 23 for blocked order on Nighthawk. At $L = 10$, the depth of the blocked circuit drops from 10 to 8. The gate counts also drop.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The ordering is a hardware decision, not a physics decision.** Blocked and interleaved order are the same Hamiltonian with\n"
"renamed modes, as the relabeling check in section 2 shows. The ordering changes which modes are neighbors, and the encoding\n"
"and the coupling map see that change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The structure is useful only with a synthesis that uses it.** With a term-by-term synthesis (`LieTrotter`), the depth on a line grows\n"
"linearly, to 599 at $L = 50$, compared with 23 for the flow-set circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**All results in this tutorial use custom syntheses.** Both circuits use a one-Clifford recipe for every flow set, so the\n"
"comparison is fair, but a different synthesis might change the numbers. Future releases of\n"
"`qiskit-fermions` might automatically find a short-depth diagonalizing Clifford circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Caveats.** The depths are for noise-free coupling maps (`FakeFez` and `FakeNighthawk`), manually built fractional targets, and\n"
"manually chosen layouts. The best of 20 routing seeds is an optimistic result by design. It is the depth that you can reach if you\n"
"route a circuit many times and keep the best result, which is inexpensive to do before a run. The median is the result of a single transpilation,\n"
"and it is never smaller than the best result. This tutorial compares structure, not fidelity. On Heron, the two-ancilla blocked variant is routed without a manual layout,\n"
"because the heavy-hex lattice has no ladder. A better placement might do somewhat better than the router, but\n"
"the lattice still has no ladder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<TutorialFeedback />\n"
msgstr ""
